2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-433027381-1-501602/isaac-itzig-moshe-netter-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-6b29676ab3d0f88fc629df641d85d8b7&mrid=675ed41c10798ebee27ff03266201865 MyHe
2 CONC ritage Family Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 16 2020, 1:13:18 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Itzig, born around 1670, died in 1733, married around 1687 to Marion Heimendinger, was the real founder of the community of Rosheim. He built the synagogue in 1720, as well as the family home on the "New Jewish Street" which becam
2 CONC e "Rue Netter". He had two sons. The eldest Lippmann (1698-1772) married to Frumat Hertz of Mannheim, continued his father's action, faced with an always hostile magistrate, multiplying charitable and community institutions, hospi
2 CONC ces, hospitals for the elderly and sick, schools for all levels (yeshiva). The youngest, Lehmann (1709-1792), was an entrepreneur " der Königlichen Fourrage ", banker, supplier of the armies, finally General Officer of the Jewish 
2 CONC Nation in Alsace. He had married in 1729 Beyle, the daughter of Moyse David Oppenheimer, Court Jew in Mannheim, in second marriage, Fraidel Elias, of Bingen.
2 CONT Thanks to their relations with the men in power, the Comte d'Argenson, the Duc de Choiseul, the General Officers of the Jewish Nation Moyse Blien, Aaron Mayer, Jacob Baruch Weyl, Lehmann Netter and Cerf Berr had succeeded in impro
2 CONC ving the political condition and social status of their co-religionists, to ensure them a certain intellectual advancement and relative security. Of all their actions, the most spectacular was the trip to Versailles by Aaron Mayer
2 CONC  and Lehmann Netter, during which they succeeded in obtaining the referral to the Parliament of Metz of the criminal case against Hirzel Lévy and his co-accused, condemned to death by the Sovereign Council of Alsace (1755). Mayer 
2 CONC Netter (1730-1805), son of Lehmann, experienced the Revolution and the ruin of the family. He tried to maintain the community during these difficult years. He had done extensive rabbinical studies. Detached from material things, h
2 CONC e did not succeed in regaining his ancestral fortune, which was left in assignats, but his conduct was exemplary in the face of the new ideas conveyed by the Revolution.
