1 OCCU préposé des Juifs de Rosheim
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Préposé des juifs de Rosheim. Face à un magistrat hostile, il multiplia les institutions charitables et communautaires et fonda des hospices, écoles et hopitaux pour malades.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-40001-51209885/lippman-netter-in-familysearch-family-tree FamilySearch Family Tree] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 16 2020, 1:06:14 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lippmann (1698-1772) married to Frumat Hertz of Mannheim, continued his father's action, faced with an always hostile magistrate, multiplying charitable and community institutions, hospices, hospitals for the elderly and sick, sch
2 CONC ools for all levels (yeshiva). The youngest, Lehmann (1709-1792), was an entrepreneur " der Königlichen Fourrage ", banker, supplier of the armies, finally General Officer of the Jewish Nation in Alsace. He had married in 1729 Bey
2 CONC le, the daughter of Moyse David Oppenheimer, Court Jew in Mannheim, in second marriage, Fraidel Elias, of Bingen.
2 CONT Thanks to their relations with the men in power, the Comte d'Argenson, the Duc de Choiseul, the General Officers of the Jewish Nation Moyse Blien, Aaron Mayer, Jacob Baruch Weyl, Lehmann Netter and Cerf Berr had succeeded in impro
2 CONC ving the political condition and social status of their co-religionists, to ensure them a certain intellectual advancement and relative security. Of all their actions, the most spectacular was the trip to Versailles by Aaron Mayer
2 CONC  and Lehmann Netter, during which they succeeded in obtaining the referral to the Parliament of Metz of the criminal case against Hirzel Lévy and his co-accused, condemned to death by the Sovereign Council of Alsace (1755). Mayer 
2 CONC Netter (1730-1805), son of Lehmann, experienced the Revolution and the ruin of the family. He tried to maintain the community during these difficult years. He had done extensive rabbinical studies. Detached from material things, h
2 CONC e did not succeed in regaining his ancestral fortune, which was left in assignats, but his conduct was exemplary in the face of the new ideas conveyed by the Revolution.
