1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT <p style="text-align: left;" dir="ltr">&nbsp;<a href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/wolff/">http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/wolff/</a></p>
2 CONT <p><div id="biography">of 1794. Serving with the Army of the Rhine, in October of 1795 he was serving at Mannheim when he was wounded by a shot to the thigh during the night and taken as a prisoner of war. Wolff was released from 
2 CONC captivity six months later in April of 1796. Three years later in 1799 he was commissioned as a sous-lieutenant in the 5th Dragoons. He served in Belgium that year and then in 1800 he was promoted to lieutenant and served in the A
2 CONC rmy of the West.</p>
2 CONT <p><p class="pIndent">In 1803 Wolff was sent to serve in the Army of the Coasts and he became an aide-de-camp to <a title="Louis Bonaparte" href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/bonaparte2/" data-img-src="/static/images/of
2 CONC ficers/small/bonaparte2.jpg">Louis Bonaparte</a>. The next year he was promoted to capitaine and he joined the 18th Dragoons. In 1805 Wolff joined the staff of the Cavalry Reserve and he served in this role for the next few years.
2 CONC  During the Prussian campaign of 1806 he was promoted to chef d'escadrons in November, and then in February of 1807 he was wounded at Ostrolenka. At the end of 1807 Wolff was authorized to pass to the service of the newly created 
2 CONC Kingdom of Westphalia. While serving in Westphalia Wolff organized the cavalry, served in the Royal Guard of <a title="J&eacute;r&ocirc;me Bonaparte" href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/bonaparte3/" data-img-src="/static
2 CONC /images/officers/small/bonaparte3.jpg">J&eacute;r&ocirc;me Bonaparte</a>, and was promoted to colonel. In 1809 he led a brigade of Westphalian cavalry to join the Army of Germany to assist the French in the Danube campaign of 1809
2 CONC . In 1811 Wolff was named a Knight of the Order ofthe Crown of Westphalia.</p>
2 CONT <p><p class="pIndent">In April of 1812 Wolff was promoted to g&eacute;n&eacute;ral de brigade and he took command of the 1st Brigade of Westphalian Cavalry in the VIII Corps of the Grande Arm&eacute;e. He and his men served in the
2 CONC  Russian campaign but when King J&eacute;r&ocirc;me left the army that August Wolff accompanied him. In early 1813 Wolff was named an aide-de-camp to J&eacute;r&ocirc;me Bonaparte. That March he commanded the troops defending Harz
2 CONC  and then in April he took command of the light cavalry of <a title="Auguste-Fr&eacute;d&eacute;ric-Louis Viesse de Marmont" href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/marmont/" data-img-src="/static/images/officers/small/marmo
2 CONC nt.jpg">Marshal Marmont's</a> VI Corps. In July of 1813 Wolff took command of the 29th Light Cavalry Brigade composed of Westphalians, Bavarians, and Hessians that served as part of <a title="Nicolas-Charles Oudinot" href="http://
2 CONC www.frenchempire.net/biographies/oudinot/" data-img-src="/static/images/officers/small/oudinot.jpg">Marshal Oudinot's</a> XII Corps. At the end of August Wolff fought near the village of Feldheim where he was wounded by a shot to 
2 CONC the right shoulder. <a title="Napoleon Bonaparte" href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/napoleon/" data-img-src="/static/images/officers/small/napoleon.jpg">Napoleon</a> sent Wolff back to serve with his brother J&eacute;r
2 CONC &ocirc;me and the two of them returned to France in October. Wolff served in command of a brigade of light cavalry, finally commanding the 28th Brigade of the 6th Light Cavalry Division under <a title="Charles-Claude Jacquinot" hr
2 CONC ef="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/jacquinot/" data-img-src="/static/images/officers/small/jacquinot.jpg">General Jacquinot</a>.</p>
2 CONT <p><p class="pIndent">After Napoleon's abdication in April of 1814, the restored Bourbons backdated Wolff's service and named him a mar&eacute;chal de camp of France from November 7th, 1813, and they named him a Knight of Saint Lo
2 CONC uis. However, they they also put Wolff on non-activity. When Napoleon returned from exile to resume power for the Hundred Days of 1815, Wolff again became an aide-de-campto J&eacute;r&ocirc;me Bonaparte. He served with J&eacute;r&
2 CONC ocirc;me in the 6th Division of <a title="Honor&eacute;-Charles-Michel-Joseph Reille" href="http://www.frenchempire.net/biographies/reille/" data-img-src="/static/images/officers/small/reille.jpg">Reille's</a> II Corps in the Army
2 CONC  of the North during the short campaign that June. Afterwards Wolff was sent on a mission to Orl&eacute;ans and then put on non-activity.</p>
2 CONT <p></div>
