1 OCCU Grand Rabbin, président du Grand Sanhédrin de Napoléon
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} né à Trêves vers 1745 alors que son père y était rabbin
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand rabbin d'Alsace, président du Grand Sanhédrin de Napoléon réuni en 1807
2 CONT
2 CONT GenAmi 40
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT See he book at:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hebrewbooks.org/15248
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_David_Sinzheim
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph David Sinzheim (1745 – February 11, 1812, Paris) was the chief rabbi of Strasbourg. He was son of Rabbi Isaac Sinzheim of Treves, and brother-in-law of Herz Cerfbeer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography[edit] Sinzheim was the most learned and prominent member of the Assembly of Notables convened by Napoleon I on May 30, 1806. The task of answering the questions laid before the assembly by the imperial commissioner was e
2 CONC ntrusted to Sinzheim, who fulfilled his duties (July 30-August 3, 1806) to the satisfaction of the assembly as well as of the commissioner and even of Napoleon himself. The German sermon which he delivered in the synagogue of Pari
2 CONC s in honor of the emperor's birthday, on Aug. 15, also strengthened Napoleon's favorable opinion of the Jews, who received the imperial promise that their rights as French citizens should not be withdrawn.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 9, 1807, four days after the Assembly of Notables was dissolved, the Grand Sanhedrin was convened; its chairman ("nasi"), appointed by the minister of the interior, was Sinzheim, who had probably suggested the assembly
2 CONC , having been frequently consulted by the imperial commissioner. The consistorial constitution, provided by the decree of March 17, 1808, opened a new field of activity for Sinzheim, who was elected chairman of the Central Consist
2 CONC ory. He was regarded as the foremost French Talmudist of his time, and was the author of the Yad David, which has recently been printed in its entirety by Machon Yerushalayim.
