1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The surname SCHWABE is a German and Ashkenazic Jewish regional name for someone from Swabia. The name was derived from the German word SCHWABEN, so called from a Germanic tribe recorded from the Ist century BC in t
2 CONC he Latin form SUEBI or SUEVI. This region in south Germany was an independent duchy from the 10th century until 1313, when the territory was broken up. (Because of the close relationship between the English and German languages, s
2 CONC ome Germans are able to transform their names to the English form just by dropping a single letter. Many Germans have changed their names in America. A great number of immigrants from Germany settled in Pennsylvania. After the sta
2 CONC rt of the first World War, Germans in great numbers Anglicized their names in an effort to remove all doubt as to their patriotism. Afterwards some changed back, and then during World War II the problem became acute once more, and
2 CONC  the changing started all over again, although not with as much intensity.)
2 CONT
