1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  George Blumenthal was a banker, philanthropist, and art collector. He was one of the original members of the Knollwood Club on Lower Saranac Lake, occupying Cottage Two. He conveyed his membership to his cousin, H
2 CONC ugo Blumenthal.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Frankfort-am-Main, Germany, and came to the United States in his youth. For twenty-one years he was a senior partner of Lazard Frères where he was known as one of the ablest financiers of Wall Street. He retired fro
2 CONC m active banking in 1925 having amassed a fortune from foreign currency exchange. He devoted the remainder of his life to art and medicine, in particular, the American Hospital in Paris and the Mount Sinai Hospital in New York, wh
2 CONC ere he served as President from 1910-1938. In 1928 he gave $1 million to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and was its President from 1934 until his death in 1941. He also contributed heavily to the New York Public Library and the S
2 CONC orbonne, in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT He built several mansions, including two in New York City, a townhouse in Paris, and a château in Grasse, near Cannes, France. In 1903, he had a mansion built at 23 West 53rd Street described by Christopher Gray as "one of the bes
2 CONC t mid-block mansions ever erected in New York." For many years, this mansion was used as the bookstore of the Museum of Modern Art.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blumenthal built another showplace mansion at 50 East 70th Street on the southwest corner of Park Avenue, that was filled with his collection of art; it featured a large interior patio complex that was originally in the abandoned 
2 CONC castle of Vélex Blanco in Spain. Many of his art masterpieces were bequeathed to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and the famous patio is now on prominent exhibit next to the grand staircase at the Metropolitan Museum.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the uncle of Katharine Graham publisher of the Washington Post.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT     George Blumenthal Residence
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Financier and President of the Metropolitan Museum of Art from 1934 until his passing in 1941. A german foreign-exchange banker, George Blumenthal was the head the U.S. branch of Lazard Frères, an investment banking firm centered 
2 CONC in New York City. Described as a "difficult man with a big ego", Mr. Blumenthal was one of five bankers who saved Grover Cleveland from giving up specie payments in 1896, with their $65,000,000 gold loans. He returned to the New Y
2 CONC ork Stock Exchange in 1916, and retired from active banking in 1925. Known throughout as a dedicated art lover, Mr. Blumenthal had a passion for period  artwork and architecture pieces. At his New York City residence ~ "Velvex Bla
2 CONC nco" named after the Spanish castle ~ Mr. Blumenthal installed a grand interior patio from that castle, which he later willed to the Metropolitan Museum of Art in 1941. The mansion, occupying an entire block, also had installed a 
2 CONC  Mediterranean tiled indoor pool in the basement. As a philanthropist, George Blumenthal summered with his wife in France, at their Parisian residence or their chateau in Grasse, near Cannes, France, which included "Salle Gothique
2 CONC ", a separate wing built especially for their Casavant Frères organ, where Mr. and Mrs. served on the boards of the American Foundation for French Art and Thought, the Children's Hospital in Paris and the American Hospital in Pari
2 CONC s. For their works and donations, the couple was given the French Legion of Honor in 1929. For seven years, till his death, Mr. Blumenthal served as president of the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York and also president of the
2 CONC  Mount Sinai Hospital, New York. It had long been Mr. Blumenthal's hope that his "Velvex Blanco" could serve as a wing to the Museum after his passing, though he realized the impossibility of this, and left a clause in his will st
2 CONC ating the mansion was to be demolished after his death. Mrs. Blumenthal passed away in 1930, and afterwards, the widowed Blumenthal married the former Marion Clew of Newport and New York.The two later endowed the George and Marion
2 CONC  Blumenthal Research Scholarships awarded annually for demonstrated merit in community arts leadership by the Roski School of Fine Arts at the University of Southern California.
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=122637987 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 30 2016, 5:25:05 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT George Blumenthal was a German-born banker who served as the head of the U.S branch of Lazard Frères.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born to a German Jewish family in Frankfurt am Main, George Blumenthal a foreign-exchange banker who had been sent to the United States by Speyer & Co., rose to prominence as the head of the U.S branch of Lazard Frères and was a p
2 CONC artner of Lazard Frères in France. Blumenthal was president of the Mount Sinai Hospital, New York, where he donated $2mil and where the Blumenthal auditorium is named after him. He was a trustee of the Metropolitan Museum of Art f
2 CONC or many years as well as president of the American Hospital of Paris. He served as the seventh president of the Metropolitan Museum of Art from 1934 until his death in 1941, where he gave $1mil and to which he bequeathed the Patio
2 CONC  from the Castle of Vélez Blanco, a colonnaded Spanish Renaissance patio. He retired from Lazard in 1901, giving up his seat on the stock exchange, and returned as a partner in 1906. He returned to the stock exchange in 1916, purc
2 CONC hasing a seat for $63,000. With J. P. Morgan the elder, he was one of five bankers who saved Grover Cleveland from giving up specie payments in 1896, with their $65,000,000 gold loans.
2 CONT
2 CONT His niece, Katharine Graham, in her memoir Personal History, described her uncle as a "difficult man with a big ego." He and Florence also named the Blumenthal Rare Book and Manuscript Library, which contains rare and illustrated 
2 CONC books, manuscripts, Haggadot, as a resource for scholarly research.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Florence Blumenthal, George Blumenthal married Mrs. Mary (Marion) Clews, the former Miss Mary Ann Payne of New York, and widow of James Clews, banker — in December 1935 at age 77. The two later endowed the Georg
2 CONC e and Marion Blumenthal Research Scholarships awarded annually for demonstrated merit in community arts leadership by the Roski School of Fine Arts at the University of Southern California.
