1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florence_Meyer Florence Meyer Homolka (January 22, 1911 – November 27, 1962) was an American portrait photographer and socialite. She was the wife of the actor Oskar Homolka.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [show]  Early life[edit] She was born Florence Meyer in New York City, the eldest daughter of Eugene Meyer (1875–1959), publisher of the Washington Post, and Agnes Elizabeth (Ernst) Meyer (1887–1970). Her younger sister 
2 CONC was the future Washington Post publisher Katharine Graham. Her father was Jewish and her mother was Lutheran, from a family of German descent.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT She studied dance and acting in Paris and Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Photography[edit] She photographed numerous artists, playwrights, actors, writers, composers, musicians, statesmen, film stars, and other celebrities of her day. Her work included portraits of James Agee, Thomas Mann, Constantin B
2 CONC râncuși, Charlie Chaplin, Judy Garland, Vladimir Horowitz, Lion Feuchtwanger and of fellow photographers Edward Steichen, Walker Evans, and Brassaï.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Meyer was a close friend and protégé of Man Ray, and in 1946 took the photographs for the double wedding portraits of Man Ray and Juliet Browner, and Max Ernst and Dorothea Tanning. Meyer was the author of "Focus on Art", publishe
2 CONC d posthumously in 1963.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life[edit] In 1939, Meyer married the Austrian character actor Oskar Homolka (1898–1978). They had two sons, Vincent and Laurence, and later divorced.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Homolkas moved to Los Angeles around 1943, and lived at 10788 Bellagio Road in Bel-Air and 914 Corsica Drive in Pacific Palisades.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Los Angeles in 1962. Her body was cremated.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Florence Meyer Homolka was an American portrait photographer and socialite. She was the wife of the actor Oskar Homolka.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born Florence Meyer in New York City, the eldest daughter of Eugene Meyer (1875–1959), publisher of the Washington Post, and Agnes Elizabeth (Ernst) Meyer (1887–1970). Her younger sister was the future Washington Post publ
2 CONC isher Katharine Graham. Her father was Jewish and her mother was Lutheran, from a family of German descent. Along with her four siblings, she was baptized as a Lutheran but attended an Episcopal church.
2 CONT
2 CONT She studied dance and acting in Paris and Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT She photographed numerous artists, playwrights, actors, writers, composers, musicians, statesmen, film stars, and other celebrities of her day. Her work included portraits of James Agee, Thomas Mann, Constantin Brâncuși, Charlie C
2 CONC haplin, Judy Garland, Vladimir Horowitz, Lion Feuchtwanger, Arnold Schoenberg and of fellow photographers Edward Steichen, Walker Evans, and Brassaï.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meyer was a close friend and protégé of Man Ray, and in 1946 took the photographs for the double wedding portraits of Man Ray and Juliet Browner, and Max Ernst and Dorothea Tanning. Meyer was the author of "Focus on Art", publishe
2 CONC d posthumously in 1963.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1939, Meyer married the Austrian character actor Oskar Homolka (1898–1978). They had two sons, Vincent and Laurence, and later divorced.
2 CONT
2 CONT After leaving Germany shortly after the Nazis took power, the Homolkas lived in London for a few years. They then settled in Los Angeles around 1943, and lived at 10788 Bellagio Road in Bel-Air and 914 Corsica Drive in Pacific Pal
2 CONC isades.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Los Angeles in 1962. Her body was cremated.
