1 NOTE [hemmerdinger.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin and Schonele lived in Jungholtz, Alsace at the time of their marriage.  She was the daughter of Leopold (Jehuda Leib ben Josel s'l') Salomon of Biescheim and Ella bas Meir Netter.  Benjamin ben Joseph ben Aron of Scherwil
2 CONC ler had his brother Eisik mentioned as given rights to "Chaliza" in his marriage contract.  The bride brought a dowry of 3,000 Reichsthaler.  The young couple had the right to eat for four years "am Familientisch."  The bride was 
2 CONC assured of receiving half of a male heir. In Nov., 1784, Benjamin was listed in the census of Jews of Alsace as the Rabbi in Niedernheim (Niedernai).  The information on his second wife and children is taken from that document and
2 CONC  also from a letter of Pierre Lautmann, Nov., 1995.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin is buried in the Jewish cemetery of Rosenwiller, Section III, grave 10.  The inscription reads: "Rabbi Benjamin Hemmerdinger,  Rabbin du Territoire de la Noblesse Immediate de Basse Alsace a Niedernai, 1771 a 1783."  Info
2 CONC rmation on this gravesite from a letter of Robert Veil of Versaille, 28 Nov., 1988.  Additional information from Raphael & Weyl's book, "Regards Nouveau sur les Juifs d'Alsace."  Rosanne and Daniel Leeson visited this grave in the
2 CONC  summer of 1995 and photographed it.  A photo may also be found in the book "Le Cemetiere Juif de Rosenwiller" of Robert Wey.
