1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emile_Durkheim   '''Émile Durkheim'''] was born in Épinal in Lorraine, coming from a long line of devout French Jews; his father, grandfather, and great-grandfather had been rabbis. At
2 CONC  an early age, he decided not to follow in his family's rabbinical footsteps. Durkheim himself would lead a completely secular life. Much of his work, in fact, was dedicated to demonstrating that religious phenomena stemmed from s
2 CONC ocial rather than divine factors. While Durkheim chose not to follow in the family tradition, he did not sever ties with his family or with the Jewish community. Many of his most prominent collaborators and students were Jewish, a
2 CONC nd some were blood relations. The exact influence of Jewish thought on Durkheim's work remains uncertain; some scholars have argued that Durkheim's thought is in fact a form of secularized Jewish thought, while others argue that p
2 CONC roving the existence of a direct influence of Jewish thought on Durkheim's achievements is difficult or impossible.
2 CONT
2 CONT A precocious student, Durkheim entered the École Normale Supérieure (ENS) in 1879. The entering class that year was one of the most brilliant of the nineteenth century and many of his classmates, such as Jean Jaurès and Henri Berg
2 CONC son would go on to become major figures in France's intellectual history.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emile_Durkheim
