2 CONT #Générale##Générale##Générale##Générale#Durkheim (Émile) (Épinal, 1858 ­ Paris,1917), sociologue français. Influencé par le positivisme, il visa à établir le caractère scientifique de la sociologie     ; il définit l'objet et lesm
2 CONC éthodes decette discipline (Règles de laméthode sociologique, 1894),dont la fécondité futillustrée par ses travaux (De la division du travail social, 1893     ; le Suicide, 1897     ; les Formes élémentaires dela vie religieuse: l
2 CONC e système totémique en Australie
2 CONT , 1912). I
2 CONT l fonda, en 18
2 CONT 96,la revuel'Année sociologique, autour de laquelle se constitua l'écolefrançaise desociologie.© Hachette Multimédia / Hachette Livre, 1999Emile Durkheim (1858-1917) is by far one of the most important and prolific sociologists in
2 CONC  the history of the f
2 CONT ield. Durkheim himself is credited withmaking sociology a science, as he usedan emp
2 CONT irical methodology in hisown studies, espe
2 CONT cially in regard tohis study of suiciderates andissues of European nations.
2 CONT Durkheim coined the term"anomie," and shed light on the inner workings of society that his predecessors had overlooked. He showed that all the aspects ofhuman society worktogethermuch like the parts of a machine, and thisconcept i
2 CONC sref
2 CONT erred to today as sociological functionalism. This ideaof functionalism--societal organization playing the major role in thelives ofhumans--has become the very paradigm of most sociological study today.
2 CONT This site was created as a resource for undergraduate students of sociology,and should be used accordingly. Durkheim's own words are provided extensivelyin this archive, to best convey his ideas, concepts, andtheories.
2 CONT Welcome to The Emile Durkheim Archive![bs.MalkaCahen.FTW]
2 CONT
