1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bay Area civic leader Elizabeth Lilienthal Gerstley dies at 94 Carl Nolte, Chronicle Staff Writer Published 4:00 am, Tuesday, October 16, 2007 0
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Lilienthal Gerstley, a civic leader who was a member of an old California family, died Oct. 8 after a brief illness. She was 94.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Gerstley was the widow of James Mack Gerstley, a mining executive and patron of the arts, who died earlier this year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Gerstley, a descendant of the Forty-Niners who came to California in the Gold Rush, was born in San Francisco in 1913; her parents were Samuel and Alice Lilienthal. She was the second of three children. As usual in that era, 
2 CONC she was born at home - in this case the celebrated Haas-Lilienthal House at 2007 Franklin St., a building considered by many to be the acme of San Francisco's Victorian houses.
2 CONT
2 CONT She attended the Burke School, Stanford University and Smith College before her marriage to James Gerstley in 1934. James Gerstley had a long career as an executive in the mining business and was one of the developers of Boraxo, a
2 CONC  cleansing product made of Borax mined near Death Valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Gerstley was active in her own right and worked tirelessly in such causes as Planned Parenthood, the League of Women Voters, the Nature Conservancy, the Filoli Gardens in Woodside, and many others. She lived for many years on
2 CONC  the San Francisco Peninsula.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1973, the family presented the Haas-Lilienthal House to San Francisco Architectural Heritage.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by son James of Los Angeles, daughter Anne Pieper of Concord, a granddaughter and two great-granddaughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Services will be private. Contributions may be made in her name to the Haas-Lilienthal House, 2007 Franklin St., San Francisco, CA 94109; Pets in Need, 873 Fifth Ave., Redwood City, CA 94063; or the Nature Conservancy, 4245 N. Fai
2 CONC rfax Drive, Suite 100, Arlington, VA 22203.
