1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Theodore (Ted) Max Lilienthal A fourth-generation San Franciscan from one of the Bay Area's oldest families, died of cancer on December 28, 2009 at his home in Smith Ranch in San Rafael. He was surrounded by his lo
2 CONC ving family, and died two weeks prior to his 98th birthday. Ted was born in 1912 in San Francisco. He spent summers with his extended family and his great-grandparents, Louis and Hannah Gerstle, on the grounds of what is now Gerst
2 CONC le Park in San Rafael. Ted helped the head gardener, Cesar Bettini, care for the grounds and animals. He often hitched a wagon to the family horse, Beauty, and rode her into downtown San Rafael. The family donated Gerstle Park to 
2 CONC the city of San Rafael. In 1929, Ted's grandmother took his family to Europe for three months. She financed the trip by selling stock and thus avoided the great market crash. The experience taught Ted a lesson, which he often repe
2 CONC ated: "If you want to do something, get out there and do it." Ted attended Stanford University, where he earned his block S on the tennis team, and graduated in 1934 (he attended his 75th reunion in October). He moved to New York 
2 CONC to work for his uncles' business, Aris Gloves, and there he met his wife Wendy. The two married in 1941 at the Carlisle Hotel. From the day they met until his death, he doted on her, describing her as "beautiful" and "incredible,"
2 CONC  and commenting that he couldn't think of anyone he would rather spend his life with. Ted worked in a variety of sales and executive advertising positions. He first retired in his 70's after his employer expressed concerns about h
2 CONC is longevity, and returned to work for 20 more years at KQED's San Francisco Focus Magazine and San Francisco Magazine (where he worked for Steven Dinkelspiel). In 2003 he was awarded the "Older Worker Award" by Governor Gray Davi
2 CONC s. He retired in 2004 at the age of 92. Ted's passions were travel and the arts. He and Wendy held season tickets at the San Francisco Symphony. They traveled extensively, visiting art and music venues throughout the world. He was
2 CONC  an avid tennis and football fan, and loved watching Stanford games on TV with his much-loved dogs. He also maintained an autograph collection started in the 1800's by his great-grandfather, the Rabbi Dr. Max Lilienthal. The colle
2 CONC ction included personal letters, notes, and signatures of prominent scholars, artists, musicians, and world leaders. Above all, Ted treasured his wife and family. For over 60 years, he and Wendy hosted gatherings at their Sleepy H
2 CONC ollow house for all occasions, large and small. He orchestrated several Gerstle-Sloss reunions, each of which drew over 100 people. Ted also served on the boards of the Marin County Boy Scouts, the Marin Symphony, and Mended heart
2 CONC s, where he volunteered every weekend for 15 years. Ted is survived by his wife of 68 years, Wendy Lilienthal, a well-known artist. He is also survived by his son, Tom Lilienthal of La Cruz de Huanacaxtle, Mexico; and his daughter
2 CONC s, Peggy Linden of Rockville, MD and Vicky Lilienthal (Felix Rivera) of Albany, CA. He also leaves five grandchildren, Windy Linden Creath (Cory), Tate Linden (Sarah), Todd Lilienthal, Sophia Rivera, and Olivia Rivera, and three g
2 CONC reat-grandchildren, Cameron Creath, Colton Theodore Creath, and Theodore Linden. He is preceded in death by his brother Robert Lilienthal. The family is grateful for the love and exceptional care provided by Ted's caregivers, Meli
2 CONC  and Vaciseva Ganilau. In lieu of flowers, contributions in Ted's memory may be made to KQED or to Stanford University. A small, family celebration of Ted's life has already been held.
2 CONT
2 CONT Published in Marin Independent Journal on Jan. 6, 2010 
2 CONT
2 CONT - See more at: http://www.legacy.com/obituaries/marinij/obituary.aspx?pid=138207576#sthash.gW0ZSLyV.dpuf- See more at: http://www.legacy.com/obituaries/marinij/obituary.aspx?pid=138207576#sthash.gW0ZSLyV.dpuf
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Theodore (Ted) Max Lilienthal A fourth-generation San Franciscan from one of the Bay Area's oldest families, died of cancer on December 28, 2009 at his home in Smith Ranch in San Rafael. He was surrounded by his loving family, an
2 CONC d died two weeks prior to his 98th birthday. Ted was born in 1912 in San Francisco. He spent summers with his extended family and his great-grandparents, Louis and Hannah Gerstle, on the grounds of what is now Gerstle Park in San 
2 CONC Rafael. Ted helped the head gardener, Cesar Bettini, care for the grounds and animals. He often hitched a wagon to the family horse, Beauty, and rode her into downtown San Rafael. The family donated Gerstle Park to the city of San
2 CONC  Rafael. In 1929, Ted's grandmother took his family to Europe for three months. She financed the trip by selling stock and thus avoided the great market crash. The experience taught Ted a lesson, which he often repeated: "If you w
2 CONC ant to do something, get out there and do it." Ted attended Stanford University, where he earned his block S on the tennis team, and graduated in 1934 (he attended his 75th reunion in October). He moved to New York to work for his
2 CONC  uncles' business, Aris Gloves, and there he met his wife Wendy. The two married in 1941 at the Carlisle Hotel. From the day they met until his death, he doted on her, describing her as "beautiful" and "incredible," and commenting
2 CONC  that he couldn't think of anyone he would rather spend his life with. Ted worked in a variety of sales and executive advertising positions. He first retired in his 70's after his employer expressed concerns about his longevity, a
2 CONC nd returned to work for 20 more years at KQED's San Francisco Focus Magazine and San Francisco Magazine (where he worked for Steven Dinkelspiel). In 2003 he was awarded the "Older Worker Award" by Governor Gray Davis. He retired i
2 CONC n 2004 at the age of 92. Ted's passions were travel and the arts. He and Wendy held season tickets at the San Francisco Symphony. They traveled extensively, visiting art and music venues throughout the world. He was an avid tennis
2 CONC  and football fan, and loved watching Stanford games on TV with his much-loved dogs. He also maintained an autograph collection started in the 1800's by his great-grandfather, the Rabbi Dr. Max Lilienthal. The collection included 
2 CONC personal letters, notes, and signatures of prominent scholars, artists, musicians, and world leaders. Above all, Ted treasured his wife and family. For over 60 years, he and Wendy hosted gatherings at their Sleepy Hollow house for
2 CONC  all occasions, large and small. He orchestrated several Gerstle-Sloss reunions, each of which drew over 100 people. Ted also served on the boards of the Marin County Boy Scouts, the Marin Symphony, and Mended hearts, where he vol
2 CONC unteered every weekend for 15 years. Ted is survived by his wife of 68 years, Wendy Lilienthal, a well-known artist. He is also survived by his son, Tom Lilienthal of La Cruz de Huanacaxtle, Mexico; and his daughters, Peggy Linden
2 CONC  of Rockville, MD and Vicky Lilienthal (Felix Rivera) of Albany, CA. He also leaves five grandchildren, Windy Linden Creath (Cory), Tate Linden (Sarah), Todd Lilienthal, Sophia Rivera, and Olivia Rivera, and three great-grandchild
2 CONC ren, Cameron Creath, Colton Theodore Creath, and Theodore Linden. He is preceded in death by his brother Robert Lilienthal. The family is grateful for the love and exceptional care provided by Ted's caregivers, Meli and Vaciseva G
2 CONC anilau. In lieu of flowers, contributions in Ted's memory may be made to KQED or to Stanford University. A small, family celebration of Ted's life has already been held. Published in Marin Independent Journal on Jan. 6, 2010
