0 @I6000000010534100463@ INDI
1 NAME Joseph /Seligmann/
2 GIVN Joseph
2 SURN Seligmann
2 _MARNM Seligman
1 NAME Joseph //
2 GIVN Joseph
2 SURN Seligmann
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 NOV 1819
2 PLAC Baiersdorf
2 ADDR
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 APR 1880
2 PLAC New Orleans
2 ADDR
3 STAE LA
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Banker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph Seligman (1819–1880) was a prominent U.S. banker and businessman. He was born in Baiersdorf, Germany, emigrating to the United States when he was 18. With his brothers, he started a bank, J. & W. Seligman & 
2 CONC Co., with branches in New York, San Francisco, New Orleans, London, Paris and Frankfurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the post-Civil War Gilded Age, J. & W. Seligman & Co. invested heavily in railroad finance, in particular acting as broker of transactions engineered by '''Jay Gould'''. They underwrote the securities of a variety of companies,
2 CONC  participating in stock and bond issues in the railroad and steel and wire industries, investments in Russia and Peru, the formation of the '''Standard Oil Company''', and shipbuilding, bridges, bicycles, mining, and a variety of 
2 CONC other industries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877, Seligman was involved in the most publicized antisemitic incident in American history up to that point, being denied entry into the Grand Union Hotel in Saratoga Springs, New York. The case became a national cause célèbre
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Arrival in America
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 J. & W. Seligman & Co. and railroads
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 United States economy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 6 Seligman-Hilton affair
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 6.1 Background
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 6.2 The incident
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 6.3 Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 7 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 8 Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 9 References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 10 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT As a small child, Seligman worked in his mother's dry goods shop. Present-day Germany consisted of many independent states in the early 19th century, most of which issued their own, differing coinages; and young Joseph made a prof
2 CONC it at his mother's store changing money for travelers for a small fee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph's father wanted him to enter the family wool business, but circumstances made this difficult; in particular, migration of the peasant class (Seligman's father's customers) from rural areas to urban meant a loss of job oppor
2 CONC tunities and a shrinking economic base in Baiersdorf.
2 CONT
2 CONT At fourteen, Seligman attended the University of Erlangen. At seventeen, he boarded a steamer at Bremen and sailed to America.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Arrival in America
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman initially settled in Mauch Chunk, Pennsylvania, where he went to work as a cashier/clerk for Asa Packer, who later become a United States congressman. His salary was $400.00 a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Using his savings from work, Seligman began selling goods door to door in rural Pennsylvania (jewelry, knives, smaller goods), saving outlying farmers the trouble of coming into town to buy their goods. After saving $500, Seligman
2 CONC  was able to send to Germany for his brothers William and James, who joined him in peddling.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Seligmans encountered some antisemitic abuse in their interactions with Americans, though they were not discouraged from continuing to sell.
2 CONT
2 CONT  J. & W. Seligman & Co. and railroads
2 CONT
2 CONT Seilgman's firm made a number of investments in railroads. Among these were the Missouri Pacific, the Atlantic and Pacific Railroad, the South Pacific Coast Railroad, and the Missouri-Kansas-Texas Railroad. They also helped financ
2 CONC e New York's first elevated railway.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Seligmans tended to lose money on their railroad ventures. One example is buying land in Arizona that was to be used for grazing cattle, which could then be transported on the Atlantic and Pacific Railroad line. However, the a
2 CONC ridity of Arizona made it unsuitable for the venture. ''~• but read about '''Seligman''' in https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seligman,_Arizona ''
2 CONT
2 CONT  Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT During the American Civil War, Seligman was responsible for aiding the Union by disposing of $200,000,000 in bonds "a feat which W. E. Dodd said was 'scarcely less important than the Battle of Gettysburg.'"[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later historians have suggested that Seligman's role in financing the war through bonds has been exaggerated. According to Stephen Birmingham, Seligman was obliged to accept "7.30 bonds" from the Union Government as payment for th
2 CONC e uniforms his factory was delivering. Union defeats (combined with a suspiciously high interest rate) lowered confidence in the bonds, making them difficult to sell.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] United States economy
2 CONT
2 CONT President Ulysses S. Grant, who had befriended Jesse Seligman when he was a First Lieutenant near Watertown, New York, offered Joseph Seligman the post of United States Secretary of the Treasury, which he declined, possibly due to
2 CONC  shyness. George Sewall Boutwell accepted the position and eventually clashed with the Seligmans.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877, President Rutherford Hayes asked Seligman, August Belmont, and a number of other New York bankers to come to Washington, D.C., to plan a refinancing of the war debt. Each banker submitted a plan, but Secretary of the Trea
2 CONC sury Sherman accepted Seligman's plan as being the most practical. It involved retaining gold reserves totaling forty percent of circulating greenbacks through bond sales.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Seligman-Hilton affair
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1877, Judge Henry Hilton, the manager of the Grand Union Hotel in Saratoga, New York, denied entry to Joseph Seligman and his family because they were Jews, creating nationwide controversy. It was the first antisemitic incident
2 CONC  of its kind in the United States to achieve widespread publicity.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Background
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 1870s, several incidents made Alexander Stewart hostile towards Seligman, although the two men had served together on the board of the New York Railway Company (whose president was Judge Henry Hilton), a Tweed Ring asso
2 CONC ciate.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first incident involved Seligman's declining the post of Secretary of the Treasury. Stewart, who was a friend of President Grant, was then offered the post. However, because he was associated with Henry Hilton and Hilton, with
2 CONC  Tammany Hall, the Senate declined to confirm him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seligman was invited to serve in the Committee of Seventy, a group of New Yorkers who banded together to fight the Tweed Ring. Stewart's company, in retaliation, stopped doing business with Seligman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart died in 1876, having placed Hilton in charge of his estate, the largest American fortune recorded to that date. The estate included a two-million-dollar stake in the Grand Union Hotel in Saratoga, as well as A. T. Stewart'
2 CONC s department store on Astor Place. Hilton himself was unhappy with Seligman, as he was annoyed that Seligman had not invited him to a dinner given for Grant after he became president.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT  The incident
2 CONT
2 CONT After helping refinance the war debt in Washington, Seligman decided to vacation with his family at the 834-room Grand Union Hotel in Saratoga, where he had stayed before. Saratoga at the time was a well-regarded resort area for u
2 CONC pperclass New Yorkers, and the Grand Union Hotel itself was the best available.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, by 1877 the hotel had suffered a drop in business. Stewart and, after his death, his manager Hilton believed that the cause of the decline was the presence of "Israelites" (that is, Jews) at the hotel; Christians, th
2 CONC eir theory went, did not wish to stay at a hotel that admitted Jews. Seligman was told he could not stay at the hotel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historians disagree as to whether the Seligman family were physically turned away from the hotel, told not to come to the hotel, or advised that they could stay only one final time. However, it is clear that the Seligmans were mad
2 CONC e to feel that their presence at the hotel was not desired and would not be tolerated long, if at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT The incident created much controversy. The New York Times, on June 19, 1877, ran a headline set entirely in capital letters:
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         A SENSATION AT SARATOGA.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                     _____
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         NEW RULES FOR THE GRAND UNION.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         NO JEWS TO BE ADMITTED--MR. SELIGMAN,
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         THE BANKER, AND HIS FAMILY SENT AWAY--
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         HIS LETTER TO MR. HILTON--
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         GATHERING OF MR. SELIGMAN'S FRIENDS
2 CONT
2 CONT                                         AN INDIGNATION MEETING TO BE HELD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Hilton released a letter saying, "As [yet] the law . . . permits a man to use his property as he pleases, and I propose exercising that blessed privilege, notwithstanding Moses and all his descendants object."[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The case became a national sensation. Seligman and Hilton both received death threats. A group of Seligman's friends started a boycott against A. T. Stewart's, eventually causing the business to fail; a sale to John Wanamaker foll
2 CONC owed.[5] This prompted Hilton to pledge a thousand dollars to Jewish charities, a gesture mocked by the satirical magazine Puck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hilton was also castigated by Henry Ward Beecher (who knew Seligman) in a sermon entitled "Gentile and Jew." After praising Seligman's character, Beecher said, "When I heard of the unnecessary offense that has been cast upon Mr. S
2 CONC eligman, I felt no other person could have been singled out that would have brought home to me the injustice more sensibly than he."[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether or not Seligman meant to be turned away from the hotel to cast a light on growing antisemitism in America, the resulting publicity emboldened other hoteliers to exclude Jews, placing advertisements saying "Hebrews need not
2 CONC  apply" and "Hebrews will knock vainly for admission."[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Seligman's siblings were, in order of birth, William (born Wolf), James (born Jacob), Jesse (born Isaias), Henry (born Hermann), Leopold (born Lippmann), Abraham, Isaac, Babette, Rosalie, and Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married his cousin Babet Steinhardt in a ceremony in Baiersdorf in 1848. Together, they had five sons, David, George Washington, Edwin Robert Anderson Seligman, Isaac Newton Seligman, and Alfred Lincoln, as well as four daughte
2 CONC rs, Frances, Sophie and two others.
2 CONT
