2 CONT * Burial:	July 19 1921 - Cimetière de Montparnasse (14e), Paris, Île-de-France, France
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish Chronicle - July 29 1921
2 CONT
2 CONT MR. DAVID COBLENTZ (Paris).
2 CONT
2 CONT French Jewry has sustained a great loss by the death, at the venerable age of eighty-three, of Mr. David Coblentz, Honorary President of the Synagogues' Administrative Council. Mr. Coblentz, who was born at Idxheun (?), in Lorrain
2 CONC e, spent his early youth in San Francisco, and on returning established himself at Mulhouse. In 1890, he retired from business and devoted the remainder of his life to an active participation in communal and charitable work. There
2 CONC  was practically no institution of importance with which he was not connected. The Charities Committee, the Home for the Aged, the Rothschild Orphanage, the Technical School, and the Home for War Orphans, ail received the benefit 
2 CONC of his valuable administrative ability, it was, however, in connection with the general oversight of the synagogues at Paris that he was best known. He was regarded as the "Parnass par excellence." He was esteemed and beloved by a
2 CONC ll for his charm of manner, his high-mindedness, and the assiduity with which he devoted himself to his responsible position. As President of the Committee on Religious Propaganda, his personal example and character Bad no little 
2 CONC influence in bringing back many back-sliders to Judaism. Mr. Coblentz did not allow old age to dim his ardour. lie was a conspicuous figure at the. services at the synagogue in the Rue de la Victoire, and bore with exemplary piety
2 CONC  and true patriotism the terrible loss In the war of his son and two grandsons. His funeral was attended by the leading representatives of French Jewry, and speeches in eulogy of the character and work of the deceased were deliver
2 CONC ed by the Chief Rabbis of France and Paris," Mr. Emile Deutsch de la Mearthe (?) and others.
