1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Excerpts from "Rabbi Jacob Nieto of Congregation Sherith Israel, Part II" in Western States Jewish History, vol. 18, no. 2, p. 166-167'''
2 CONT
2 CONT By all accounts [Jacob Nieto's and Rose Frankel's relationship] was a true love match. Nieto had met Rose Frankel at a boarding house where he also resided for a short period. The bride had had scarlet fever as a child, and was un
2 CONC able to hear. She communicated either by reading lips or with a pad and pencil. It is patent that a woman with such a handicap could not take her position in synagogue life. At the beginning of her marriage, she regularly came to 
2 CONC services. Subsequently she appeared at the end of the service to greet members of the congregation, and toward the end of her life she did not come to the services at all. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage had its full share of tragedy. The oldest son, Frank, born in 1895, was drowned in Lake Lagunita near Stanford University on April 9 [1910], and the youngest boy, John, born in [1917], died in [1939]. He suffered from
2 CONC  Down's Syndrome. He was originally kept at the family home at 3933 Clay Street, but later was placed with a private family in Redwood City. When Rabbi Nieto compiled his High Holy Day prayer book (approved by the board on January
2 CONC  5, 1910), he omitted the verse which deals with those who shall die by drowning. Benjamin Liederman, cantor of Sherith Israel, told the writer (about 1932) that Rabbi Nieto had said he did not want to be reminded of the tragic de
2 CONC ath of his son. It is no wonder that Mrs. NIeto sought solace in the Christian Science religion. This faith was very popular during the early part of the century, and many Jews accepted the idea that pain, suffering and illness we
2 CONC re merely errors and illusions. While Anita Nieto Sufrin is adamant that the interest of her mother in Christian Science "was of no lasting interest and that it had no influence upon our family's life," other members of the congre
2 CONC gation recall that it was a matter of common concern.
2 CONT
2 CONT The interest of Mrs. Nieto in Christian Science waned, and during the 1920s Mrs. Doris Stamper recalls seeing Mrs. Nieto with her two daughters attending High Holy Day services and wondering what benefit the lady received from the
2 CONC  ritual. It was common knowledge that she was unable to hear. Mrs. Stamper recalls she was a very stately and beautiful woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Rabbi Nieto in March 1930, the congregation set aside the sum of $15,000 to be paid the widow during her lifetime, at the rate of $200 per month, commencing April 1, 1930. The last payment was make in October 19
2 CONC 37, and Mrs. Nieto wrote to the congregation thanking them for the consideration that had been shown to her. Mrs. Nieto died on March 30, 1945, fifteen years after her husband.
