3 CTRY France
1 OCCU historien, orientaliste, écrivain, conservateur Bibliothèque Mazarine à Paris, Explorateur - Historien - Orientaliste - Ecrivain - Conservateur Bibliothèque Mazarine à Paris
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A9on_Cahun
2 CONT
2 CONT David Léon Cahun (23 June 1841, Haguenau, Alsace – 30 March 1900, Paris) was a French traveler, Orientalist and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [show]  Life[edit] Cahun's family, who came originally from Lorraine, destined him for a military career. However, owing to family affairs he was compelled to relinquish this, and he devoted himself to geographical and h
2 CONC istorical studies. In 1863 he began to publish a series of geographical articles and accounts of his travels in Egypt and neighboring countries in the Revue Française. About the same time he published letters of travel, and a geog
2 CONC raphical review which was the first of its kind in the daily press. In 1864 Cahun set out to explore Egypt, Nubia, the western coast of the Red Sea, and Asia Minor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to France in 1866, he became a political writer on the staff of La Liberté. When that paper supported the Empire, Cahun left it, joining the staff of La Réforme (1869) and La Loi. During the Franco-Prussian War he was a 
2 CONC correspondent for several papers. On 4 September 1870, he entered the army as a volunteer, and was appointed sublieutenant of the 46th Foot the following November. When peace was established he resumed his Oriental studies, devoti
2 CONC ng himself chiefly to research concerning the Turks and the Tatars.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875 he was appointed to the Bibliothèque Mazarine, where he was specially engaged in the compilation of an analytical catalogue from the year 1874. Meanwhile, Cahun had begun to publish a series of historical novels dealing wi
2 CONC th ancient history, in the style of the journeys of Anacharsis in Greece. They are said by one critic to be written in temperate and pure French, combining interest with genuine archeological knowledge. It was Cahun's intention to
2 CONC  present facts of ancient history that were not generally known, and thus make contributions to general history and geography. These novels include: Les Aventures du Capitaine Magon, on Phoenician explorations one thousand years b
2 CONC efore the common era (Paris, Hachette, 1875); La Bannière Bleue, the adventures of a Muslim, a Christian, and a pagan at the time of the Crusades and the Mongolian conquest (ib. 1876); Les Pilotes d'Ango, dealing with French histo
2 CONC ry in the sixteenth century (ib. 1878); Les Mercenaires, set during the Punic Wars (ib. 1881); Les Rois de Mer, on the Norman invasions (Chasavay, 1887); Hassan le Janissaire, on Turkish military life in the sixteenth century (cro
2 CONC wned by the French Academy); La Tueuse, scenes from the Mongol invasion of Hungary in the thirteenth century (1893). Cahun contributed many literary articles to the Revue Bleue, Le Journal des Débats, etc., and several critical, g
2 CONC eographical, and ethnographical papers to the Bulletin de la Société d'Ethnographie, Bulletin de la Société Académique Indo-Chinoise Bulletin de la Société Japonaise, Bulletin de la Société Americaine, Bulletin de l'Athénée Orient
2 CONC al, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1878 Cahun set out on a fresh series of journeys accompanied by his wife. The two intrepid travelers visited central Syria, the mountains of Ansairi (1878), the Faroe Islands and Iceland (1879), central Syria and Mesopotamia (1
2 CONC 880). In 1879 the Tour du Monde published an account of his travels through Syria and the mountains of Ansairi. He also issued a volume treating the same subject, entitled Excursions sur les Bords de l'Euphrate (Paris, 1884). His 
2 CONC scholarly study of local customs, Scènes de la Vie Juive en Alsace, with preface by Zadoc Kahn, chief rabbi of Paris, appeared about the same time (ib. 1885). In 1884 he published Le Congo, la Véridique Description du Royaume Afri
2 CONC cain, Traduite pour la Première Fois en Français sur l'Edition Latine Faite par les Frères de Bry en 1598, d'Après les Voyages Portugais et Notamment Celui d'Edouard Lopez en 1578 (Brussels, 1884).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1890 Cahun established a course of lectures at the Sorbonne, where he taught the history and the geography of Asia. An abstract of one section of this course was incorporated in the Histoire Générale of Lavisse and Rambaud. Cah
2 CONC un's Introduction Générale à l'Histoire de l'Asie (1896), based on material gathered during his travels, is a complete and exact history of that continent. He also undertook the restoration of some ancient casts that are of great 
2 CONC geographical interest. Some years before his death Cahun ceased writing for the Parisian periodicals, but to the end he contributed to Le Phare de la Loire. He left an unfinished history of the Arabs, and a historical novel dealin
2 CONC g with the same topic. He was a member of several learned societies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family[edit] Cahun came from a distinguished family, who traced back its ancestry to the times of Louis IX of France,.[1] His nephew, the son of Mathilde Cahun, Marcel Schwob (1867–1905), was a prolific French writer. French photo
2 CONC grapher and writer Claude Cahun (born Lucy Schwob, 1894–1954) was his great-niece.
2 CONT
2 CONT Influences on Turkish nationalism[edit] Cahun's novel La Bannière bleue (1877) acted as a major source of inspiration for Turkish nationalist current in the Ottoman Empire and his history work Introduction à l'histoire de l'Asie: 
2 CONC Turcs et Mongols des origines à 1405 (1896) had great impact on the nationalistic historiography of the Republican era.[2] Kemal Atatürk, the founder of the Republic had also been an avid reader of Cahun's Introduction à l'histoir
2 CONC e de l'Asie.[3]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Events 23 June 1841 :	Birth - Haguenau, 67180, Bas Rhin, Alsace, France  Enfant naturel reconnu
2 CONT
2 CONT Witness : Jean WEILL, 39 ans, Colporteur Witness : Antoine OHLMANN, 39 ans, Garde de police Sources: Acte Naissance n° 145 --- :	Occupation - Conservateur adjoint Bibliothèque Mazarine à Paris - Orientaliste - Historien - Ecrivain
2 CONC  - Erudit - Professeur Libre à la Sorbonne  En sa qualité de bibliothècaire de la Mazarine, il fut logé, de 1875 jusqu'à sa mort, au Palais de l'Institut Quai de Conti à Paris VIème. Explorateur, Orientaliste : "Excursions sur les
2 CONC  Bords de l'Euphrate" "Les Ansaries" ... Historien et Ecrivain : "La Vie Juive" "La Bannière Bleue" "Les Pilotes d'Ango" "La Tueuse" "Hassan le Janissaire" "Les Mercenaires" "Les Aventures du Capitaine Magon" "Les Rois de Mer" ...
2 CONC  Erudit ; "Introduction à l'Histoire de l'Asie" ... Ami du poète parnassien José Marie de Hérédia (Recueil "Les Trophées") 30 September 1873 :	Marriage (with Palmire (Palmyre) STRAUSS) - Paris 4ème  Witness : Henri MAY 1806-1885 W
2 CONC itness : Moïse STERNS, 47 ans, Graveur Witness : Marie-Joseph Etienne JUNCA, 39 ans, Homme de lettres Witness : Edmond Bernard MOREAU, 31 ans, Capitaine d'Artillerie Sources: Acte Mariage n° 806 30 March 1900 :	Death - Paris 6ème 
2 CONC  Sources: Acte Décès n° 707 --- :	Burial - Cimetière Montparnasse à Paris ( Vème Division)  Relevé personnes Inhumées dans tombeau Cimetière Montparnasse section israélite + inscriptions :
2 CONT
2 CONT " 1- David Léon Cahun conservateur adjoint de la Bibliothèque Mazarine Professeur libre à la Sorbonne né le 23 juin 1841, décédé le 30 mars 1900
2 CONT
2 CONT 2- A la Mémoire de René CAHUN Lieut-Colonel d'Inf Coloniale Officier de la légion d'Honneur Croix de Guerre avec Palmes Mort pour la FRANCE le 16 avril 1917 à l'âge de 46 ans Inhumé à Paris au Cimetière de l'Est
2 CONT
2 CONT 3- Mme Vve Léon CAHUN née Palmyre STRAUSS le 14 février 1849 - 10 février 1933
2 CONT
2 CONT 4- Mme Henri MAY née Jeannette CREHANGE décédée le 30 mai 1865 à l'âge de 65 ans
2 CONT
2 CONT 5- Mme STRAUSS née Henriette LEVY décédée le 5 janvier 1872 à l'âge de 53 ans
2 CONT
2 CONT 6- Marcel SCHWOB né à Chaville le 23 août 1867 décédé à Paris le 26 février 1905
2 CONT
2 CONT 7- Madame CAPELLE née Cécile STRAUSS à Haguenau le 21 novembre 1858 décédée à Paris le 9 avril 1905
2 CONT
2 CONT 8- Mme Julie HEKKING-CAHUN 12 janvier 1877 - 31 décembre 1949
2 CONT
2 CONT 9- Jean ROTIL 28 juin 1906 - 2 décembre 1968
2 CONT
2 CONT 10- Marguerite CAHEN 1883 - 1973 "
2 CONT
